      "writtenOn": "2026-06-01T18:32:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In `fpsensor_test_utils.h`, if an invalid `capture_type` is provided, the helper prints an error and returns:\n\n```cpp\nif (capture_type \u003c 0 || static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(capture_type) \u003e\u003d cache.frame_sizes_.size()) {\n    printk(\"Error: Invalid capture type %d\\n\", capture_type);\n    return;\n}\n\n```\n\nWhile this mimics the legacy `CPRINTF` behavior from the production code, this is now exclusively a **test utility**. Test utilities should fail loudly. If a test passes an invalid bounds parameter to a setup helper, it indicates a bug in the test itself, and the test should immediately abort rather than silently returning and potentially causing cascading false positives/negatives.\n\n**Suggestion:** Replace `printk` with a Zephyr `zassert` macro. This will explicitly fail the test execution and provide better test reliability.\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003czephyr/ztest.h\u003e\n\n// Inside the set_frame_size method:\nzassert_true(capture_type \u003e\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(capture_type) \u003c cache.frame_sizes_.size(),\n             \"Invalid capture type %d provided to test helper\", capture_type);\n\ncache.frame_sizes_[static_cast\u003csize_t\u003e(capture_type)] \u003d size;\n\n```",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 18,
        "startChar": 0,
